==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|processioun}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|pourciession}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|processio||a marching forward, an advance, in Late Latin a religious procession}}, from {{m|la|procedere}}, past participle {{m|la|processus||to move forward, advance, proceed}}; see {{m|en|proceed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/pɹəˈsɛʃən/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|pro|ces|sion|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-procession.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[progressing]] or [[proceeding]].
#: {{rfquotek|Bishop Pearson}}
#* Trench
#*: That the '''procession''' of their life might be / More equable, majestic, pure, and free.
# A [[group]] of people or things moving along in an [[orderly]], stately, or solemn manner; a train of persons advancing in order; a [[retinue]].
#: ''a '''procession''' of mourners; the Lord Mayor's '''procession'''''
#* Shakespeare
#*: the townsmen on '''procession'''
# A number of things [[happening]] in [[sequence]] (in [[space]] or in [[time]]).
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical|obsolete|in the plural}} [[litany|Litanies]] said in procession and not kneeling.
#: {{rfquotek|Shipley}}

